text:
Learning Goals

distinguish commercial wearables from research-grade accelerometers;
describe the signals produced by a tri-axial accelerometer;
recognize why device, placement, and sampling configuration matter;
identify the device families used by major cohort studies.

Key Ideas

accelerometers measure acceleration, not behavior labels;
raw data are time-indexed signals;
gravity, movement, and orientation all affect the observed signal.

Learning Goals

identify signs of implausible or low-quality data;
distinguish wear from non-wear periods;
understand why calibration matters;
flag files or intervals that should not be analyzed as-is.

Key Ideas

poor input quality propagates through the pipeline;
wear-time decisions define what data are analyzable;
QC rules should be explicit and documented.

Learning Goals

summarize activity over complete days;
derive sleep-related summaries;
compare day and night behavior outputs;
build participant-day summaries across domains.

Key Ideas

daily summaries depend on consistent day boundaries;
sleep and activity outputs must be aligned carefully;
different behavior domains can be summarized in one analysis-ready table.

Learning Goals

import raw accelerometry data;
inspect timestamps and axis columns;
identify metadata needed for later processing;
verify that the imported structure is correct.

Key Ideas

import is part of analysis, not a neutral step;
time handling must be checked early;
structure inspection prevents downstream errors.

Learning Goals

choose appropriate accelerometry outcomes for analysis;
distinguish participant-day and participant-level models;
recognize confounding and measurement decisions;
connect preprocessing choices to final inference.

Key Ideas

modeling begins after careful outcome definition;
preprocessing choices shape the estimand;
analysis-ready summaries still require clear interpretation.

Learning Goals

explain what an epoch is;
compare outputs under different epoch lengths;
assess how aggregation changes temporal detail;
understand how behavior classification depends on summarized inputs.

Key Ideas

aggregation is a modeling choice;
coarse summaries can hide short bursts of behavior;
classification results depend on the time scale of the inputs.

Learning Goals

define valid days;
define participant inclusion rules;
count retained versus excluded observations;
document analysis cohort construction.

Key Ideas

inclusion rules change analytic results;
valid-day definitions must be prespecified;
cohort construction should be auditable.

Learning Goals

organize a complete accelerometry project;
record package versions and assumptions;
separate raw, intermediate, and final outputs;
audit the full pipeline from import to analysis.

Key Ideas

reproducibility is part of the analysis;
QC should be visible at every stage;
a course website can double as an auditable project record.

Learning Goals

define ENMO, MAD, and activity counts;
compare how each metric responds to the same raw signal;
understand what preprocessing choices affect each metric;
interpret differences between metric families.

Key Ideas

different metrics answer slightly different questions;
derived summaries depend on preprocessing rules;
metric choice affects comparability across studies.

Decisions to Record

device model, firmware, placement, dynamic range, and sampling frequency;
calibration method, parameters, and before/after error diagnostics;
files excluded or flagged because calibration was not credible;
whether later metrics use calibrated, resampled, or filtered data.

The following module derives signal metrics from these explicitly defined inputs.

Reproducibility Checklist

retain the original .gt3x and .cwa files without modification;
keep reader, calibration, metric, epoch, and non-wear settings in code and output metadata;
record R and Python package versions plus the Python environment used by reticulate;
produce an inclusion table from raw files to analytic participants;
rerun the full pipeline from a clean project environment.

Daily Summaries
For each valid day, summarize the selected metric over eligible wear minutes. Examples include mean ENMO, total minutes above a threshold, MIMS per day, count-based activity volume, sleep duration, and step count. A day-level summary must retain its denominator: number of eligible minutes or hours of wear.

daily &lt;- minute_data |&gt;
  dplyr::filter(wear, valid_day) |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(participant_id, date) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(
    mean_enmo_mg = mean(enmo_mg),
    active_minutes = sum(enmo_mg &gt;= 100),
    wear_minutes = dplyr::n(),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

One Value Per Participant
A common participant-level outcome is the unweighted arithmetic mean of that person’s valid daily summaries. It represents an estimate of the person’s average day among observed valid days. Each retained day contributes equally; a day with more wear does not receive more weight simply because it contains more observed minutes.

participant &lt;- daily |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(participant_id) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(
    mean_daily_enmo_mg = mean(mean_enmo_mg),
    valid_days = dplyr::n(),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

This estimand intentionally does not account for the number of valid days in the mean itself. The number of days remains an important precision and QC variable and should be reported, used in sensitivity analyses, or incorporated into a different estimand when warranted.

From Minutes to Valid Days
First classify each minute as wear or non-wear. Then calculate daily wear time and apply a prespecified threshold, such as at least 10 hours of waking wear time. A valid-day indicator is an analytic eligibility flag, not a claim that the day is complete or representative.

day_qc &lt;- minute_data |&gt;
  dplyr::mutate(date = as.Date(time)) |&gt;
  dplyr::group_by(participant_id, date) |&gt;
  dplyr::summarise(
    wear_minutes = sum(wear),
    valid_day = wear_minutes &gt;= 10 * 60,
    .groups = "drop"
  )
